// Birth of Bahá'u'lláh: 50 questions (35 easy, 10 medium, 5 hard). Drafts and fact-check notes: drafts/birth-bahaullah/.
// Birth and family
{"type":"single","q":"In which city was Bahá'u'lláh born?","choices":["Shíráz","Ṭihrán","Iṣfahán","Tabríz"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'u'lláh was born Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí in Ṭihrán, Persia, on 12 November 1817, into a noble family.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh was born in 1817.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was born on 12 November 1817. Bahá'ís celebrate His birth each year on a date set by the lunar calendar, so it moves from year to year.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the name Bahá'u'lláh means \"Glory of God\".","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"\"Bahá'u'lláh\" means \"the Glory of God\". He took the name Bahá, meaning glory, in 1848.","source":"bahai.org — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"What was Bahá'u'lláh's father's name?","choices":["Mírzá Yaḥyá","Mírzá Músá","Mírzá Buzurg","Mírzá Mihdí"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"His father was Mírzá Buzurg, a nobleman who served as a vizier and as governor of Burujird. Mírzá Yaḥyá and Mírzá Músá were Bahá'u'lláh's brothers, and Mírzá Mihdí was His son.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Family of Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"What was Bahá'u'lláh's name at birth?","choices":["Siyyid ʻAlí-Muḥammad","Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí","Mullá Ḥusayn","Mírzá Muḥammad-ʻAlí"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was born Mírzá Ḥusayn-ʻAlí Núrí. Siyyid ʻAlí-Muḥammad was the Báb, Mullá Ḥusayn was the Báb's first disciple, and Mírzá Muḥammad-ʻAlí was a son of Bahá'u'lláh.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"Who was the mother of Bahá'u'lláh?","choices":["Ásíyih Khánum","Fáṭimih Khánum","Khadíjih Khánum","Bahíyyih Khánum"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"His mother was Khadíjih Khánum. Ásíyih Khánum and Fáṭimih Khánum (Mahd-i-ʻUlyá) were His wives, and Bahíyyih Khánum was His daughter.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Family of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"Which nickname did Bahá'u'lláh earn as a young man?","choices":["Friend of the Orphan","Helper of the Needy","Father of the Poor","Servant of the Sick"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He gave His time and money to people in need and became known as \"the Father of the Poor\".","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these describe Bahá'u'lláh as a young man? (select all)","choices":["He declined a government post","He gave generously to those in need","He accepted the Báb's message","He served as governor of Burujird"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He turned down a government post, was generous to the poor, and accepted the Báb's message at about 27. His father, not He, was governor of Burujird.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were wives of Bahá'u'lláh? (select all)","choices":["Ásíyih Khánum","Khadíjih Khánum","Mahd-i-ʻUlyá","Sárih Khánum","Gawhar Khánum"],"answer":[0,2,4],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Ásíyih Khánum (the Navváb), Mahd-i-ʻUlyá (Fáṭimih) and Gawhar Khánum were His wives. Khadíjih Khánum was His mother and Sárih Khánum His sister.","source":"Bahaipedia — Family of Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were children of Bahá'u'lláh? (select all)","choices":["ʻAbdu'l-Bahá","Mírzá Yaḥyá","Bahíyyih Khánum","Mírzá Músá","Mírzá Mihdí"],"answer":[0,2,4],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"ʻAbdu'l-Bahá, Bahíyyih Khánum and Mírzá Mihdí were His children. Mírzá Yaḥyá and Mírzá Músá were His brothers.","source":"Bahaipedia — Family of Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Mírzá Yaḥyá was a son of Bahá'u'lláh.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mírzá Yaḥyá (Subḥ-i-Azal) was Bahá'u'lláh's half-brother, about thirteen years younger.","source":"Bahaipedia — Family of Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
// Recognition of the Báb, the Síyáh-Chál and exile
{"type":"single","q":"How old was Bahá'u'lláh when He accepted the message of the Báb?","choices":["About 17","About 22","About 27","About 33"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"He was about 27 when Mullá Ḥusayn brought Him the Báb's message and He accepted it.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"Who brought the Báb's message to Bahá'u'lláh?","choices":["Quddús","Mullá Ḥusayn","Ṭáhirih","Mírzá Yaḥyá"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Mullá Ḥusayn, the first to believe in the Báb, brought the Báb's message to Him in Ṭihrán.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: as a young man, Bahá'u'lláh accepted a government post.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He declined a post offered by the vizier Ḥájí Mírzá Áqásí and chose to spend His time helping others.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which year was Bahá'u'lláh imprisoned in the Síyáh-Chál?","choices":["1844","1848","1852","1863"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"In 1852 He was chained in the Síyáh-Chál (\"Black Pit\"), a dungeon in Ṭihrán, for about four months.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these are true of the Síyáh-Chál? (select all)","choices":["It was a dungeon in Ṭihrán","He was held there in chains","He received a divine vision there","He was held there for over a year"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"In 1852 He was chained in the Síyáh-Chál in Ṭihrán and received a vision of a Maiden through whom His mission was revealed. He was held for about four months.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: after the Síyáh-Chál, Bahá'u'lláh was freed and allowed to stay in Persia.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was banished from Persia in 1853 and travelled to Baghdád with His family.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were places of exile for Bahá'u'lláh? (select all)","choices":["Baghdád","Ṭihrán","Constantinople","Adrianople"],"answer":[0,2,3],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was banished to Baghdád, then Constantinople, then Adrianople, and finally ʻAkká. Ṭihrán was His home city, not a place of exile.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; bahai.org — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh endured about forty years of imprisonment and exile.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"bahai.org notes that He endured some 40 years of imprisonment, torture and exile for delivering God's latest message.","source":"bahai.org — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these places in the order Bahá'u'lláh was held or lived in them, first to last.","items":["Ṭihrán","Baghdád","Constantinople","Adrianople","ʻAkká"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He lived in Ṭihrán, was banished to Baghdád in 1853, moved to Constantinople in 1863, to Adrianople later that year, and in 1868 to ʻAkká.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these moments in the early life of Bahá'u'lláh in order, earliest first.","items":["Is born in Ṭihrán","Marries Ásíyih Khánum","Learns of the Báb's message","Attends the Badasht gathering","Is imprisoned in the Síyáh-Chál"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Born 1817, married Ásíyih Khánum in 1835, accepted the Báb's message about 1844, took part in the Badasht gathering in 1848, and was held in the Síyáh-Chál in 1852.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
// Baghdád, the Declaration and the exiles
{"type":"single","q":"Which ruler invited Bahá'u'lláh to Constantinople in 1863?","choices":["Napoleon III","Sultan ʻAbdu'l-ʻAzíz","Násiri'd-Dín Sháh","Czar Alexander II"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The Ottoman Sultan ʻAbdu'l-ʻAzíz invited Him to Constantinople in 1863. Napoleon III, Násiri'd-Dín Sháh and Czar Alexander II were later addressed in Tablets.","source":"Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"In 1863, Bahá'u'lláh declared His mission to His companions in a garden outside Baghdád. On which date did His stay there begin?","choices":["12 November","22 April","29 May","21 March"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"He entered the garden on 22 April 1863 and stayed about twelve days. Bahá'ís observe this as the Riḍván festival.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these happened in 1863? (select all)","choices":["He declared His mission to His companions","He arrived in Constantinople","He arrived in ʻAkká","He arrived in Adrianople","He passed away"],"answer":[0,1,3],"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"In 1863 He declared His mission (April), reached Constantinople (August) and arrived in Adrianople (December). ʻAkká came in 1868 and His passing in 1892.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which city did Bahá'u'lláh stay for about four and a half years, arriving in December 1863?","choices":["Baghdád","Constantinople","Adrianople","ʻAkká"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He stayed in Adrianople (Edirne) until August 1868, writing Tablets to kings and rulers there.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these moments in the life of Bahá'u'lláh in order, earliest first.","items":["Is imprisoned in the Síyáh-Chál","Is banished to Baghdád","Declares His mission in a garden outside Baghdád","Reveals the Kitáb-i-Aqdas","Passes away at Bahjí"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Síyáh-Chál in 1852, banishment in 1853, declaration in 1863, Kitáb-i-Aqdas in 1873, and His passing in 1892.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these events of the Baghdád years in order, earliest first.","items":["Retreats to the mountains of Kurdistan","Returns to Baghdád","Reveals the Hidden Words","Reveals the Kitáb-i-Íqán","Goes to the Riḍván Garden"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"He left for Kurdistan in 1854 and returned in 1856. The Hidden Words date from 1857–58, the Kitáb-i-Íqán from 1862, and He left for the Riḍván Garden in April 1863.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh declared His mission to His companions in 1844.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"1844 is the year of the Báb's Declaration. Bahá'u'lláh declared His mission in 1863.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
// Writings
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these were revealed in Baghdád? (select all)","choices":["Hidden Words","Kitáb-i-Aqdas","Seven Valleys","Tablet of Carmel","Kitáb-i-Íqán"],"answer":[0,2,4],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Hidden Words, Seven Valleys and Kitáb-i-Íqán were revealed in Baghdád. The Kitáb-i-Aqdas (1873) and the Tablet of Carmel (1891) were revealed in ʻAkká.","source":"Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which city was the Kitáb-i-Aqdas revealed?","choices":["Baghdád","ʻAkká","Adrianople","Constantinople"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Bahá'u'lláh revealed the Kitáb-i-Aqdas, His \"Most Holy Book\", in ʻAkká around 1873.","source":"Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"What is the English title of the Lawḥ-i-Ḥikmat?","choices":["Tablet of Visitation","Tablet of Wisdom","Tablet of the Holy Mariner","Tablet of Aḥmad"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The Lawḥ-i-Ḥikmat is the Tablet of Wisdom, revealed in ʻAkká in 1873–74.","source":"Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these writings of Bahá'u'lláh in the order they were revealed, earliest first.","items":["Kitáb-i-Íqán","Tablet of Aḥmad","Tablet to the Kings","Kitáb-i-Aqdas","Epistle to the Son of the Wolf"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"Kitáb-i-Íqán (1862), Tablet of Aḥmad (1865), Tablet to the Kings (1867–68), Kitáb-i-Aqdas (1873) and Epistle to the Son of the Wolf (1891).","source":"Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh addressed Tablets to rulers, including Queen Victoria and Napoleon III.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"From Adrianople and ʻAkká He wrote to kings and rulers including Queen Victoria, Napoleon III, Pope Pius IX and Czar Alexander II.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Leo Tolstoy described Bahá'u'lláh's teachings as \"the highest and purest form of religious teaching\".","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Russian author Count Leo Tolstoy praised His teachings in these words.","source":"bahai.org — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"Spell the name of the Prophet-Founder of the Bahá'í Faith.","word":"Bahá'u'lláh","difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'u'lláh, \"the Glory of God\", received His mission in the Síyáh-Chál and declared it in 1863.","source":"bahai.org — Bahá'u'lláh; Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The garden outside Baghdád where He declared His mission in 1863 gives its name to a twelve-day festival.","word":"Riḍván","accept":["Ridwan"],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'ís celebrate the Riḍván festival to mark the twelve days that Bahá'u'lláh spent in this garden.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The Tablet of ___ (Lawḥ-i-Karmil), revealed in 1891, is named for this mountain.","word":"Carmel","accept":["Karmil"],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"The Tablet of Carmel (Lawḥ-i-Karmil) was revealed in ʻAkká in 1891.","source":"Bahaipedia — Writings of Bahá'u'lláh"}
// ʻAkká, Mazraʻih, Bahjí and the Ascension
{"type":"single","q":"In which year did Bahá'u'lláh arrive in ʻAkká?","choices":["1853","1863","1868","1877"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"He left Adrianople on 12 August 1868 and arrived in ʻAkká on 31 August 1868.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"Who interviewed Bahá'u'lláh at Bahjí in 1890?","choices":["Thomas Chaplin","Shaykh Ismáʻíl","Edward Granville Browne","Sayyid ʻAlí Afnán"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The Cambridge orientalist Edward Granville Browne met Him at Bahjí in 1890. Thomas Chaplin visited ʻAkká in 1871.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which places did Bahá'u'lláh live in during His final years? (select all)","choices":["Mazraʻih","Baghdád","Bahjí","Adrianople"],"answer":[0,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He lived at Mazraʻih from 1877 to 1879 and in the Mansion of Bahjí from 1879 until 1892.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"multi","q":"Which of these are true of Bahjí? (select all)","choices":["He lived in its Mansion from 1879","His Shrine stands there","It lies just outside ʻAkká","He declared His mission there"],"answer":[0,1,2],"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahjí is just outside ʻAkká. He lived in the Mansion from 1879 and is buried beside it. His mission was declared in the Riḍván Garden.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"spell","clue":"The place just outside ʻAkká where Bahá'u'lláh spent His last years and where His Shrine stands.","word":"Bahjí","difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'u'lláh lived in the Mansion of Bahjí from 1879 and was buried in a neighbouring house, now His Shrine.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these moments in the later life of Bahá'u'lláh in order, earliest first.","items":["Arrives in ʻAkká","Moves to Mazraʻih","Moves to the Mansion of Bahjí","Is interviewed by Edward Granville Browne","Passes away at Bahjí"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"medium","explanation":"ʻAkká in 1868, Mazraʻih in 1877, the Mansion of Bahjí in 1879, the Browne interview in 1890, and His passing in 1892.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"In which year did Bahá'u'lláh pass away?","choices":["1870","1879","1892","1899"],"answer":2,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He passed away at Bahjí on 29 May 1892, aged 74. Bahá'ís observe this as the Ascension of Bahá'u'lláh.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh passed away at Mazraʻih.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He died at the Mansion of Bahjí, where He had lived since 1879. Mazraʻih was His home from 1877 to 1879.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"single","q":"Whom did Bahá'u'lláh's Will name as the leader of the Bahá'í community after Him?","choices":["Mírzá Yaḥyá","ʻAbdu'l-Bahá","Shoghi Effendi","Mírzá Muḥammad-ʻAlí"],"answer":1,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"His Will, the Kitáb-i-ʻAhd, named His eldest son ʻAbdu'l-Bahá as successor and interpreter. Shoghi Effendi was His great-grandson.","source":"Bahaipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh; Brilliant Star — Bahá'u'lláh"}
// The Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh is the Qiblih for Bahá'ís' daily obligatory prayer.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"Bahá'ís face the Shrine at Bahjí when saying the daily obligatory prayer.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh; Wikipedia — Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: the Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh is surrounded by gardens.","answer":true,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"The Shrine and Mansion stand at the centre of a circular garden of olive groves and flowerbeds.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"truefalse","q":"True or false: Bahá'u'lláh was buried inside the Mansion of Bahjí.","answer":false,"difficulty":"easy","explanation":"He was buried in a neighbouring house, belonging to His son-in-law Sayyid ʻAlí Afnán. That house became the Shrine.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh"}
{"type":"order","q":"Put these events at Bahjí in order, earliest first.","items":["The Mansion is completed","Bahá'u'lláh moves into the Mansion","Bahá'u'lláh is buried beside the Mansion","Shoghi Effendi restores the Mansion","Shoghi Effendi lays out the Haram-i-Aqdas"],"answer":[0,1,2,3,4],"difficulty":"hard","explanation":"The Mansion was completed in 1870, Bahá'u'lláh moved in in 1879 and was buried in 1892. Shoghi Effendi restored it after 1929 and laid out the Haram-i-Aqdas from 1952.","source":"Wikipedia — Shrine of Bahá'u'lláh"}
